%S A398143 1,2,1,4,2,4,7,1,4,7,9,12,2,4,7,9,12,15,17,20,1,4,7,9,12,15,17,20,22,
%T A398143 25,28,30,33,2,4,7,9,12,15,17,20,22,25,28,30,33,36,38,41,43,46,49,51,
%U A398143 54,1,4,7,9,12,15,17,20,22,25,28,30,33,36,38,41,43,46
%N A398143 Survivor in the Josephus problem with golden-ratio steps: n people stand in a circle, counting starts at person 1, and the i-th person eliminated is the A282162(i)-th person counted (steps 3,2,3,3,2,3,2,3,3,2,..., the Fibonacci word with 0 -> 3, 1 -> 2).
%C A398143 An analog of A006257 (every second person eliminated) in which the Fibonacci numbers play the role of the powers of 2; see Leonov (2026).
%H A398143 Andrei Leonov, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23223471">The Josephus problem with golden-ratio steps</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A398143 If F = A087172(n) is the largest Fibonacci number <= n and j = n - F >= 1, then a(n) = floor(j*phi^2) + 2 = A001950(j) + 2.
%F A398143 a(Fibonacci(2k)) = 1 and a(Fibonacci(2k+1)) = 2 for k >= 1.
%F A398143 a(n) = n if and only if n+1 is a Fibonacci number.
%e A398143 For n = 8 the steps are 3,2,3,3,2,3,2; the people are eliminated in the order 3,5,8,4,7,6,2, so a(8) = 1.
%e A398143 For n = 30 = 21 + 9: a(30) = A001950(9) + 2 = 23 + 2 = 25.
%o A398143 (Python)
%o A398143 from math import isqrt
%o A398143 def a(n):
%o A398143     B = lambda m: m + (m + isqrt(5*m*m))//2  # A001950
%o A398143     F = [1, 2]
%o A398143     while F[-1] <= n: F.append(F[-1] + F[-2])
%o A398143     r = max(i for i in range(len(F)) if F[i] <= n)
%o A398143     j = n - F[r]
%o A398143     return B(j) + 2 if j else (1 if r % 2 == 0 else 2)
%o A398143 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 73)])
%Y A398143 Cf. A000045, A001622, A001950, A006257, A087172, A282162.
%K A398143 nonn,easy,new
%O A398143 1,2
%A A398143 _Andrei Leonov_, Oct 21 2026
